
🧩 DFDL CSV Example — Short Notes

🧠 Step 1: Sample Data

John,25,1999-01-01,Y,50000
Raj,30,1994-02-02,N
Ana,28,1996-03-03,Y,45000

🧱 Step 2: Logical Structure

Root → employees

Repeating element → employeeRecord

name → string

age → int

dob → date (YYYY-MM-DD)

permanent → boolean (Y/N)

salary → decimal (optional)





---

⚙️ Step 3: Physical Rules

Property	Value

Field Separator	,
Record Separator	%NL;
Separator Policy	trailingEmpty
Boolean Mapping	Y→true, N→false
Representation	text
Length Kind	delimited
Encoding	{dfdl:encoding} (dynamic)



---

🧩 Step 4: defaults.xsd

Defines reusable default format:

<dfdl:defineFormat name="myDefaults">
  <dfdl:format representation="text"
               encoding="{dfdl:encoding}"
               textTrim="none"/>
</dfdl:defineFormat>


---

🏗 Step 5: employees.xsd

Main schema (imports defaults + IBM extension):

<xs:element name="employees" ibm:dfdlMessage="true">
  <xs:complexType>
    <xs:sequence dfdl:separator="%NL;">
      <xs:element name="employeeRecord" maxOccurs="unbounded">
        <xs:complexType>
          <xs:sequence dfdl:separator="," 
                       dfdl:separatorSuppressionPolicy="trailingEmpty">
            <xs:element name="name" type="xs:string"/>
            <xs:element name="age" type="xs:int"/>
            <xs:element name="dob" type="xs:date" dfdl:textDatePattern="YYYY-MM-DD"/>
            <xs:element name="permanent" type="xs:boolean"
                        dfdl:textBooleanTrueRep="Y"
                        dfdl:textBooleanFalseRep="N"/>
            <xs:element name="salary" type="xs:decimal" minOccurs="0"/>
          </xs:sequence>
        </xs:complexType>
      </xs:element>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>
</xs:element>


